# WINDOW-EXTEND: `extend`, the shell note, and a skip that says the omission had a cause

Three things the mechanism's first hours asked for, in the file they belong to.

## `extend`, because correcting a duration must not free the box

I announced 100 minutes for a block that is running 131. The obvious correction — end and
re-announce — is the wrong one: the file cannot say "the same holder, a moment later", so between
the two calls the slot is genuinely free and another seat may take it. **A block that overran its
estimate is exactly when that must not happen.**

`extend_window(nonce, minutes)` rewrites `expected_end_epoch` in place, holder-only and
nonce-guarded like `end_window`, appending to an `extensions` list so the correction is visible
rather than silent. It prints nothing: the token is already in the holder's shell, so there is no
export line to emit. `runlock extend --minutes N` on the command line.

The test asserts the property that matters — **the file never left**, so the slot never opened —
and that a wrong token changes nothing.

## The token lives in the launching command's shell

Documented on `WINDOW_HOLDER_ENV`, because it surprised its author. A later command in a *new*
shell does not have the token, is treated as a non-holder, and has its own MLX-reaching tests
skipped. Safe direction, deliberate, and worth saying.

## A silent skip is its own hazard

**This one is not a polish.** On 2026-09-08 a reviewer ran a suite during my window, read `exit 0`,
and reported a file green that the collector had skipped entire — 71 tests. The exit code was
honest and the count was in pytest's own summary; nothing said the omission had a **cause**.

`pytest_terminal_summary` now writes a `box window` section naming the holder, the count, and the
sentence that matters: *this run did NOT exercise the files that can reach MLX; re-run after the
end line before reporting them green.* It is silent when no window is open **and when a window is
open but this run collected nothing it covers**, so it is not noise on every unrelated run made
during somebody's block.

`mark_items_for_a_foreign_window` returns the window and the count for that, rather than the
window alone.

## A latent flake, found by running the suite during a live window

Three `test_runlock` tests failed the first time a **real** window was open, because `_acquire`
consults it and the suite had no protection. `conftest` redirects `default_window_path` to a
temporary path for the whole suite, exactly as it already redirects the lock and for the same
reason — **host state must not be able to fail the suite**. The handful of tests that are *about*
where the window lives take an `unredirected_window_path` fixture and see the real function.

Nothing about this was visible until a window existed. It would have bitten the next seat to run a
suite during any block.

## Verification

```
test_runlock.py, test_repository_rules.py, test_spawn.py: 110 passed
ruff: clean on all three changed files
git apply --check against HEAD: 0
```

**Run as the window's holder under stub `mlx` and `mlx_lm`**: the token exported so the
collector let the files through, the stubs in force so nothing could map MLX beside the live
training block. The full suite runs at the block's end line.

One thing this patch cannot demonstrate live and covers by unit test instead: the summary
section firing on a real run. Doing that needs a peer's run collecting a covered file during
an open window, which is the situation the patch exists to make legible rather than one to
manufacture.

